{
  "Version": 2,
  "Name": "Warren1968-T2-HLHLHHLHLH-x2",
  "Source": "S. W. Warren, \"Properties and performance of basic designs of infrared interference filters,\" Infrared Phys. 8, 65-78 (1968). DOI not printed in the paper - Table 2, p. 67 (rule p. 67: \"an L layer has been inserted between the basic units\"); Table 3 p. 71; Table 4 p. 73",
  "Notes": "Double half-wave (multi-cavity) bandpass built by repeating basic unit HLHLHHLHLHS 2 times with an L coupling layer between units, 19 layers\nSpecification: Bandpass at lambda0 with steeper (more rectangular) edges than the single basic unit.\nReported: Table 2 (p. 67): 2 basic units of HLHLHHLHLHS: FWHM bandwidth 0.88 % of peak wavelength, ratio of tenth-peak to half-peak bandwidth 1.57. Table 3 (p. 71, double half wave, basic unit HLHLHHLHLHS*): bandwidth (no errors) 0.88 %; a thickness error of 0.82 % (first half-wave layer too thick, second too thin by the same %) reduces peak transmission by a factor 0.5, 0.57 % by a factor 0.7. Table 4 (p. 73): T at lambda0/lambda = 1 (lambda0 = 4 um) = 84.6 % with no absorption (see the -aH100/-aH200 designs for the absorbing cases).\nNotes: Design string written out: HLHLHHLHLHLHLHLHHLHLHS. H = quarter-wave optical thickness of index 4.0, L = quarter wave of index 1.8 at lambda0, S = substrate of index 1.65 (p. 66); adjacent equal letters (HH, LL) are merged into one half-wave layer of 2 QWOT. The paper writes designs from the incident (air) side to S, so the order is kept as printed. lambda0 is not fixed for Tables 1-3 and the figures (results are in lambda0/lambda); 4000 nm (4 um, as in Tables 4-6) is used, which is harmless because all indices are constant. Recomputations against the paper match only if the uncoated back surface of the n=1.65 substrate is included incoherently (substrate -> air): air/L/1.65 alone gives T = 89.4 % at lambda0 while Table 4 prints 84.6 %, and with the back surface it is 84.6 %; the rejection minima of Sec. 2.1.3 (3.45 %, 0.087 %, 0.0020 %, 0.000047 %) also match exactly only with the back surface. Normal incidence, unpolarised. Bandwidth = full width at half peak transmission, in % of peak wavelength. A check with the back surface included gave 5.49 % (paper 5.26) for HLHHLHS, 2.30 % (2.20) for HLHLLHLHS and 1.00 % (0.97) for HLHLHHLHLHS, i.e. about 4 % larger than printed; the 1968 program's definition or sampling may differ slightly.\nLayers from Table 2, p. 67 (rule p. 67: \"an L layer has been inserted between the basic units\"); Table 3 p. 71; Table 4 p. 73; layer 1 faces the incident medium.\nChecked with ThinFilmDesigner (2026-10-21), agrees with the paper: ours FWHM 0.906 % of 4 um (peak T 91.4 %); paper FWHM 0.88 % (Table 2).",
